A||N||23||Voytek||Are there any Fieros in the US?||||10||
Z||000000||Voytek||08-13-2001||04:48 PM||||Ok - I just got back from a loooong drive from Calgary, AB to Las Vegas, Nevada.  I put about 5,200 km on my '87 GT (about 3,250 miles) in 12 days.  Approximately 80% of this distance was driven in the US.  Well, get this, during the entire trip I've seen only 2 (THAT'S RIGHT, 2) Fieros - both notch-back, 4-cyl.  THAT'S IT!!!  It made me wonder at times if mine was the only Fiero on the highway at any given time!  Oh, and both of those were parked in a couple of cities - none on the road!  It's just something that made me wonder...<P>On a side note, I consider the $600 CDN spend on my AC a very good investment - the temperature in Las Vegas was 45 deg C (about 110 deg F) - HOW DO YOU PEOPLE LIVE???<BR>Oh, and to the skeptics who wondered about the new refrigerant I used in my system - it worked awesome and my butt is still in one piece, even after seeing those temperatures!<P>Oh, and I believe the Fiero is one of the most comfortable cars for long distance travel.  To-date I haven't cruised in a car where my back doesn't get achy after 8 or more hours of travel in one day.  In fact, we've driven for 15 hours one day (with breaks) and I felt comfortable all the way.<BR>I can't imagine doing this in any other car.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000001||Jay||08-13-2001||04:53 PM||||Cool!  I  once did Toronto to Miami and back to Toronto in a week-end (24hrs each way)!  Stayed long enough to do some shopping and made it back for work Monday morning.  Yes the Fiero is a comfortable car.  Voytek, here in Toronto the weather was 45 (110) all week.  You should have saved money on the exchange and came to Toronto.  We've got gambling too!<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voytek||08-13-2001||05:30 PM||||I know what you mean, Jay.  The exch. rate is a rip-off right now.  Needless to say, I didn't do much shopping (not that I could pack any more stuff into the Fiero).<P>I've seen your heatwave on TV.  Man, we've had 30 deg C here for the last couple of days since I got back and that seems hot!  I'll shop in Toronto when it cools off a bit!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||08-13-2001||05:42 PM||||There are a few Fiero's left in the US, but I think most of them have migrated up to Canada.  On two seperate trips this year I have gone from Buffalo to Detriot, and Chicago.  Both times I went thru southern Ontario and saw a bunch of Fiero's around.  On US highways, I only saw two nice GT's in Chicago, around Schaumburg.||||reg||1
Z||000004||The Fieromaster||08-13-2001||05:52 PM||||There are allot of Fiero's in Eastern US!  In Michigan alone when i went there some of the coolest kids have Fieros... KC Terwilliger, James Essar, Stan Hokestra, Joe (man i dunno your last name) aka FieroJoe, and Ken aka MooCow!!  SO its always a blast to go up there!  As for home here in Ohio.. a few buddies have them!  About 5 total.  Then there is the Fiero turbo god Steve Hammn with his 420hp 84 Fiero!!  Maybe you guys should come out here next time.. hehe!||||reg||10
Z||000005||JohnF||08-13-2001||06:00 PM||||How do we make it in Texas - GREAT!<P>I've spent 6 of the last 7 years in Ottawa and with a condo North of Montreal (St.-Sauveur-des-Monts) & Calgary and Edmonton. As a bona-fide born and bred Texan, I'll take North Texas weather (100 as I write) and drive my Fiero anytime with the roof open(and a/c on)||||reg||10
Z||000006||Voytek||08-13-2001||06:26 PM||||I hear ya JohnF - I work with a Texan here in Calgary and he's always cold.<BR>We tried driving through Utah with the A/C on and sunroof out - had to stop and put it back in, it was too hot!!  I guess I prefer piling clothes on, rather than stripping them off - you can only take off so much!<P>As far as number of Fieros - I see them in Calgary daily but have seen almost none down there.  I guess a rust proof car goes a long way in Canada (well, almost rust proof).||||reg||1
Z||000007||johnt671||08-13-2001||07:29 PM||||I know of 15 Fieros in the Hazleton PA area, including two twins for my white 84 SE.  Maybe three or four times a year I'll see one in the paper for sale, but none show up at the local car shows. Spoke to one guy and his wife at a gas station that owned 7 Fieros.||||reg||1
Z||000008||formula2||08-13-2001||09:31 PM||||I live in St-bruno, south of Montreal.Now that I have a Fiero, I see them everywhere. One neighbor has one , white 2M4.<BR>I saw another one in St-Bruno that is for sale..85 2m4 for $4450.000 CDN wich is about $50.00 US..LOL...<P> <BR>||||reg||2
Z||000009||killerb15||08-13-2001||10:30 PM||||-last weekend i went for a two hour drive and saw 7 fiero's!! 5 were on the road, one being worked on, and a purple-notchback-convertable parked in a driveway! what a great way to spend an afternoon!! as far as I see it fiero's are plentiful in Ontario! ||||reg||14
Z||000010||BC-GT||08-13-2001||10:38 PM||||I hear you man, went from Burnaby,BC to San Diego,CA to Winnipeg,MB and saw exactly one on the highway(in Seattle on the I5 in town)<BR>Didn't see a lot of anything over a few years old as a matter of fact!<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
